README-NOT-SHIPPED -- programs that were found and chose to stay outThe archives this collection was gathered from hold more than it ships.Most of what stayed behind is a duplicate, a fragment or a program thatcannot run anywhere; that is not listed.  What is listed here is anythingyou might reasonably look for and wonder about: what it is, why it is noton this disk, and where it is, so you can judge for yourself.The archive names are those of the Microware OS-9 archive and the EFFOforum disks; SOURCES.txt quotes each program's terms in full.THE AUTHOR'S TERMS DO NOT ALLOW A DISK LIKE THIS------------------------------------------------  vi          An ex/vi for OS-9/68000, adapted by Helmut Hoheisel-Zimmermann              from the XENIX sources.  Its own first line says it              "originates from the sources of vi running under the XENIX              operating system"; nobody who could grant it has.  Its              helpers go with it: expreserve and exrecover (which save and              restore a buffer when vi dies), add_errmsg and read_mail, and              the files SYS/vi_usage, SYS/vi_errmsg and SYS/.exrc.              EFFO forum disk 13, SOFTWARE/C/VI.  The vi on this disk is              PVIC, which is public domain; DOC/README-VI.  RCS 4       ci, co, rcs, rcsdiff, rcsident, rcsmerge, rlog.  The              distribution's own READ_ME is Purdue's non-disclosure form:              "RCS will not be distributed in any form or by any means              without prior written permission".  CMDS/rcs4.lha.  RCS 5 and              later are GNU-licensed.  sysmon      A system monitor.  Its source is marked "the proprietary              confidential property of MAX PLANCK INSTITUTE KERNPHYSIK              HEIDELBERG"; the author offers it to anyone, but the notice is              the institute's.  CMDS/SYSMON.lzh.  SEDT        Anker Berg-Sonne's editor (e, new_e): available "for customers              and Digital internal use on the condition that ... no              modifications are made".  EFFO forum disk 11,              SOFTWARE/C/SEDT_EDITOR.  btree, isam Softfocus BTREE and ISAM.  The forum disk lists them as public              domain; their own btree.doc says they are no longer, sold for              about $65.  EFFO forum disk 2.  tshell      A shell whose readme says "NOT Public Domain, but its free for              private use".  EFFO public-domain disk 4.  rz, sz      Omen Technology's ZMODEM, licensed at $20 a user by the order              form in the archive.  APPS/rzsz_3_36_3_OSK.lzh.  k, xy and z in              CMDS/COMMS are Tim Kientzle's free X/Y/ZMODEM.  smbfm       An SMB (Samba) client: free to copy, but "No part of this              material may be distributed with other software packages              without the express permission from the author".              NETWORK/smbfm14t.zip.  greed       "Please don't redistribute this."  Matthew T. Day, 1989.              A Usenet posting, from v_misc.ar on the hc disk.  travesty    Daniel J. Bernstein's text generator: permission ran "Until              January 1, 1994".  From Usenet.  ape does the same job here.  puzzle15, puz15, udate, uwho              Their authors allow unmodified copies only, and an OS-9 build              is a modified copy.  EFFO forum disk 16 (puzzle15), v_misc.ar              on the hc disk (puz15), the UNaXcess BBS (udate, uwho).MICROWARE'S OWN---------------Your OS-9 has these.  The collection carries only the six run-time modulesMicroware gave permission for (DOC/README-CIO, DOC/fpu.doc).  break       The system-debugger utility, in a disassembled edition from              EFFO forum disk 5.  msfm        MS-DOS file manager source, from Dibble's OS-9 Insights,              carrying Microware's notice.  EFFO forum disk 12.  cio020, fpu040              Run-time modules found inside other programs' archives.  load        Not Microware's code but a clean-room rewrite of their              utility, made in 2026 from the published manuals for this              collection's test harness.  A program named for one of              Microware's is theirs to ship, so it stays out, and your own              `load' does its job -- SYS/login puts /h1/CMDS on PATH.  Kept              in the collection's source repository, under withheld/load.FOUND, BUT THEY DO NOT WORK WITH ANYTHING HERE----------------------------------------------  pbmexec     Fifteen early PBM tools (pbmtops, pbmcrop, cbmtopbm ...).              They reject PBM files that netpbm, in CMDS/NETPBM, reads and              writes correctly.